# cargo-zempic 0.0.1

- Workspace: `[WORKSPACE_ROOT]`
- Members: 1 (1 selected)
- Profile: `release` (inherits: none)
- Optimizing for: size
- Target: `aarch64-apple-darwin`

## Tier 1: Foundational

| Rule | Severity | Magnitude | Change | Location | Effort | Runtime cost | Fix | Detail | Tradeoff |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `profile/strip` | high | large | unset -> "symbols" |  | trivial | none | safe | The symbol table and the strings it references are kept in the linked artifact even though they are not needed to run it. | Stripping removes function names from backtraces and profiler output. |
| `profile/missing-config` | medium | moderate |  |  | trivial | none | report-only | Every setting falls back to cargo's defaults, which favor compile time over a small artifact. The other findings in this report name the settings to set. | There is nothing to lose by configuring the profile. |

## Tier 2: Compiler

| Rule | Severity | Magnitude | Change | Location | Effort | Runtime cost | Fix | Detail | Tradeoff |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `profile/opt-level:size` | high | large | unset -> "z" |  | trivial | notable | consequential | The compiler optimizes for speed. Optimizing for size lets it drop code that exists only to run faster. | Runtime cost, pronounced with `"z"` on compute-heavy code. |
| `profile/lto` | medium | moderate | unset -> "fat" |  | trivial | none | consequential | Optimizing across crate boundaries removes code a per-crate build must keep. | Link time rises substantially. |
| `profile/codegen-units` | low | negligible | unset -> 1 |  | trivial | none | consequential | A single code generation unit lets `profile/lto` optimize across the whole crate; with several units, that optimization is limited. | Compiles are slower; the setting is what unlocks link-time optimization. |

## Tier 3: Behavioral

| Rule | Severity | Magnitude | Change | Location | Effort | Runtime cost | Fix | Detail | Tradeoff |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `profile/panic-abort` | low | small | unset -> "abort" |  | trivial | none | consequential | Unwinding carries the machinery that walks the stack and runs destructors. Aborting on panic lets the compiler drop it. | Aborting loses unwinding: a `catch_unwind` in the workspace or any dependency will no longer recover from a panic. The setting applies workspace-wide, because `panic` cannot be set per package. |

## Summary

- 6 findings
- 2 high
- 2 medium
- 2 low
- By magnitude: 2 large, 2 moderate, 1 small, 1 negligible
- 5 auto-fixable

Magnitude is an ordinal judgement, not a measurement. zempic does not build and does not read compiled artifacts, so it reports no sizes.
